"# Concurrency Analysis — Summary Report\n\n**History:** T1, T2 (read-modify-write {A, B}); T3 (read-only {A, B}), under snapshot isolation with MVCC.\n\n## Findings\n\n| Area | Result |\n|---|---|\n| **Isolation level** | **Snapshot Isolation** — frozen per-transaction snapshots, writes validated at commit (first-committer-wins). Not Serializable: SI's guarantee doesn't exclude write skew; this execution merely didn't trigger it. |\n| **Execution outcome** | T1 commits ✅ · **T2 aborts** ❌ (write set {A,B} intersects T1's concurrently committed write set) · T3 commits ✅ |\n| **Anomalies** | **None materialized.** Lost update on A and B was *attempted* (both writers computed from v0) and *prevented* by the T2 abort. No dirty reads (all reads consume committed versions), no non-repeatable reads (single snapshot), phantom reads N/A (no predicate reads), no write skew (write sets identical, not disjoint). |\n| **Conflicts** | T1⇄T2: 4× rw anti-dependencies + 2× ww (all dissolved by the abort). T1→T3: 2× wr (reads-from). T2↔T3: none. |\n| **Serialization graph** | Committed SG = {T1, T3}, edges T1 ──wr(A), wr(B)──▶ T3. **Acyclic.** |\n| **Serialization result** | ✅ **Serializable** — equivalent to serial schedule **T1 → T3** (identical reads-from and final state (A₁, B₁)). |\n\n## Key Caveats\n\n- The serializable verdict is **conditional on the T2 abort**. Had T2 committed, the committed graph would contain the rw cycle T1 ⇄ T2 on A and B → **non-serializable with lost updates**.\n- The workload sits one write-set change from SI's blind spot: disjoint write sets with this same read pattern (e.g., T1 writes {A}, T2 writes {B}) would commit both and produce **write skew** — SI's validator only catches direct write-write overlap.\n\n## Recommendation\n\nThe workload is safe but maximally contended — every writer touches every item, so SI admits only one writer per snapshot epoch and forces retries. If concurrent writers must both proceed, use SELECT … FOR UPDATE / write locks, or partition the write sets."